## 📂 Project Structure

```text
asmcodes/
├── hw-id.asm       # Low-level x86_64 assembly backend (CPUID & raw PCI I/O port mapping)
├── maincpuid.c     # C bridge handling iopl(3) permissions and full PCI bus/device/function scanning
├── analyze.pl      # Perl orchestration engine parsing raw output, DMI metadata, storage, and network info
└── README.md5      # Original project documentation notes

🚀 Overview
Hardware Device Auditor is a hybrid systems-level tool designed to extract raw hardware identities, 
CPU specifications, PCI device registries, motherboard DMI metadata, storage models, and 
network interfaces directly from hardware registers and kernel interfaces.

It bypasses standard high-level abstractions by talking directly to CPU instructions (CPUID) 
and hardware I/O ports (0xCF8 and 0xCFC) via C and Assembly, orchestrated seamlessly through a Perl frontend.

🛠️ Requirements & Execution
Linux (Primary Target)
Designed and tested on Linux environments with root/administrative privileges.

Execution Command:

Bash
sudo perl analyze.pl

Windows Support(NEVER TESTED IT YETT!! DONT CARE ABOUT WINDOWS ...DUHHH!!!!)
BUT---If your Windows machine has a Perl interpreter installed, 
you can execute it and try it using administrative privileges 

🔍 Features & Data Extracted
CPU Metrics: Fetches raw CPU Vendor ID and Brand strings directly via assembly CPUID leaves.
PCI Bus Scanner: Iterates through buses, devices, and functions to query vendor and 
device IDs via low-level I/O port instructions.

Motherboard DMI & BIOS Info: Pulls board vendor, name, serial number, 
BIOS version, and release date from /sys/class/dmi/id/.

Storage & Network Auditor: Scans block devices, models, MAC addresses, and interface states.

GDID Fingerprinting: Generates a unique system fingerprint string combining baseboard serial 
and primary network MAC metadata.

Hope this helps those looking and searching for hardware information and device details. 
